{Layer_Schema} :=

LAYER(
  id          := [generated layer id],
  name        := [<scope>@<content>: the address the layer is about, @, a short adjective_noun for what it holds; base is the default scope, e.g. base@best_practices, code.cpp@best_practices],
  base_id     := [base id],
  variable    := [the one question every item in the layer answers; states what the layer holds and how its values are read],
  for         := [the property or object this layer was built for, when there is one],

  elements := [
    ELEMENT(
      namespace_address := [dotted namespace address],
      role              := [AUE|CQU|DE|SE|NE],

      elements := [
        ELEMENT(
          namespace_address := [dotted namespace address],
          role              := [DE|SE|NE],
          elements          := [],
          joins             := []
        )
      ],

      joins := [
        JOIN(
          type     := purpose,
          purpose  := [RE Operative Purpose],
          ef       := [PE Encounter Function within this RE encounter],
          ifd      := [send|receive|both],
          elements := []
        ),

        JOIN(
          type    := content,
          to      := [this element, a join here, or a property; the object the content is about],
          kind    := [instructions|rules|imperatives|logic|workflow|other kind the request implies],
          name    := [which one, when several sit on the same object],
          content := [the thing],
          refs    := [
            REF(
              key   := [key],
              value := [value]
            )
          ]
        ),

        JOIN(
          type  := property,
          to    := [the object the property describes],
          value := [the property's one value]
        )
      ]
    )
  ]
)

# Notes
+> A join is an edge between two objects with a type. It is not a kind of thing. Any object may be joined to any object.
+> purpose: a function encounter, the receiver's purpose served by the elements it holds. Does not travel.
+> content: knowledge joined to the object it is about: an element, a join, a property. content is the thing; kind says how to read it; refs says where it came from. A sources item is all refs and no content. Does not travel.
+> property: an object with one value, joined to what it describes. Travels: applies toward the leaves from its anchor, visible toward the trunk, never a sibling.
+> name is the layer's address. Read in reverse with @ as "for", it speaks: code.cpp@best_practices is "best practices for cpp code".
+> The scope before @ is an address of the base, or base itself. The text after @ is free: a short adjective_noun chosen from what the request creates.
+> A layer is named for what it holds or what it is, never for what produced it.
+> variable is the one question the layer answers, so an agent can use the layer without inferring from the name.
+> Layer names are unique across all layers of the base.
+> A layer overlays the base identified by base_id using the same namespace structure.
+> The layer may contain only the portions of the base for which it contributes knowledge.
+> An element is named by its namespace_address alone; its name and name_length come from the base.
